.DS_Store
*.log
tmp/
node_modules/
.claude/worktrees/
.claude/*.lock
.claude/settings.local.json

# Derived awaiting-you queue — rewritten by each /loopd:dispatch tick from
# projects/*/tasks/*.md. A view, not source; never commit it. Delete the file
# itself to turn the queue off (the loop never recreates it).
/.ai-bridge/AWAITING.md

# Derived board snapshot — rewritten by scripts/write-snapshot.sh at the end of each
# /loopd:dispatch tick, and read by scripts/build-board.sh (possibly from another instance).
# A view, not source. Delete the file itself to take this instance off the board (the
# writer never recreates it).
/.ai-bridge/SNAPSHOT.json

# The board page is DERIVED and never tracked. It used to be committed on every tick that
# changed something — which made one path contended by every clone of a shared bundle, for
# a file both of them regenerate in a second. The board is served locally instead:
# `/loopd:board serve` binds 127.0.0.1 on a port derived from this bundle's path and
# serves it out of AB_BOARD_DIR (bundle-paths.sh), ignored below. Nothing is pushed.
/board.html

# The other-owners cache (scripts/build-board.sh) — the board's second half, read from
# the tracked documents at HEAD and stored against the SHA it was computed for, because
# that section can only change when HEAD moves. Derived and per-machine; delete it freely
# and the next render rebuilds it. /loopd:init appends this line too, so it reaches
# instances stamped before the cache existed.
/.ai-bridge/.board-others.json

# The local live board page (scripts/watch-board.sh, scripts/board-serve.sh) — the page
# regenerated on every task-document change by a watcher you keep running. Derived,
# per-machine, and safe to delete. /loopd:init appends this line too, so it reaches
# instances stamped before the directory existed.
/.ai-bridge/.board-live/

# The PM dispatch lock (scripts/tick-lock.sh) — written by /loopd:dispatch immediately before it
# dispatches a tick and released when that tick reports, so the one-tick-at-a-time
# guarantee survives a compaction instead of resting on a session's memory. PER CLONE and
# never committed: two humans sharing one bundle work from two clones and each dispatches
# independently, which a shared lock would break. Derived and safe to delete when no tick
# is running.
/.ai-bridge/.tick-lock

# The tick's claim on that lock (the second half of the same mechanism): a tick takes the
# lock too, because a resumed tick never passes through the launcher, and the claim is what
# tells "held by the launcher that dispatched me" from "held by another tick". Per clone and
# derived exactly like the lock, removed with it by `scripts/tick-lock.sh release`. Its own
# line because `/.tick-lock` does not match it and instances stamped before it exists carry
# the first line already — /loopd:init appends this one separately for that reason.
/.ai-bridge/.tick-lock.claim

# API keys — for a substituted model backend (the loopd-llm companion) or anything
# else. NEVER tracked: a key in a tracked file is a published key, and that launcher
# refuses to read a .env git already knows about rather than trusting this line alone.
.env

# Per-machine identity overrides (authorEmail, ownerGithubUser). instance.config.json
# is tracked and therefore SHARED, so on a bundle two humans clone its authorEmail
# would author both people's commits as one person. This file wins over it for those
# identity keys only. Never commit it.
instance.config.local.json

# The derived navigation indexes (`/index.md`, `/projects/*/index.md`) are ignored
# too — but the lines live in /loopd:init, NOT here, and that is not an oversight.
# THIS FILE IS ITSELF AN ACTIVE .gitignore INSIDE THE loopd TEMPLATE'S OWN
# `seed/` DIRECTORY, so a `/index.md` line here matches `seed/index.md`
# and silently stops the template from tracking its own seed file. (Measured: it
# broke the refresh-seeds.sh fixture, which re-inits a repo over a copy of seed/.) The
# other entries above are safe because no seed file is named any of them.
# /loopd:init appends both lines to an instance's .gitignore if missing, which also
# reaches instances stamped before they existed. `knowledge/index.md` is
# deliberately NOT ignored anywhere — it is the KB's curated lookup surface, it
# changes only when the KB changes, and an agent told to scan it needs it present
# in a fresh clone.

# Derived view of the group's product repos (scripts/link-repos.sh) — one symlink
# per repo into reposRoot, never content, and machine-local like the machinery
# below. Delete it freely; /loopd:init or that script recreates it.
/repos/

# NO MACHINERY BLOCK, DELIBERATELY. A bundle used to carry 37 symlinks into a
# template checkout, listed between a marker pair /loopd:init rewrote on every
# stamp. The machinery ships in the loopd PLUGIN now — per machine, not per
# bundle — so there is nothing here to ignore. A bundle stamped under the old design
# still has the block; /loopd:init RETIRES it, markers and all, on the next stamp.

# The knowledge-base mount's own git directories (scripts/kb-sync.sh mount) — nested
# clones the machinery creates and never commits. Per clone; never delete one with
# unpushed commits in it (`scripts/kb-sync.sh status` says).
/.ai-bridge/kb.git/
/.ai-bridge/kb-src/

# Copies refresh-seeds.sh keeps when it merges a seed change in — the file it replaced,
# and any merge it could not resolve, with its conflict markers. Derived and per-machine:
# nothing in the bundle tree ever carries a conflict marker, and nothing here needs
# committing. Delete it freely. (`.ai-bridge/seed-base/` beside it IS tracked — it is the
# merge base this bundle was stamped from, and it is the same on every clone.)
/.ai-bridge/refresh/

# Instance additions (kept across seed refreshes)
# Everything from the heading above to end of file is THIS bundle's own. refresh-seeds.sh
# compares the seed against the file with this block removed and re-appends it unchanged,
# so a pattern you add here never reads as seed drift. /loopd:init appends here too.
